### What is the problem?

Given an array of integers, move all zeros to the end while preserving the relative order of the non-zero elements. The operation must be done in place with O(1) extra space.

At the National Bank of India, Rajesh's daily settlement has real amounts scattered with empty (zero) slots. The reconciliation system needs all real amounts at the front in their original order.

```java
package com.bank.core;

import java.util.Arrays;

public class MoveZerosDemo {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        int[] settlements = {500, 0, 300, 0, 0, 700, 100, 0};
        System.out.println("Before: " + Arrays.toString(settlements));
        moveZeros(settlements);
        System.out.println("After:  " + Arrays.toString(settlements));
    }

    static void moveZeros(int[] arr) {
        int write = 0;
        for (int scan = 0; scan < arr.length; scan++) {
            if (arr[scan] != 0) {
                arr[write++] = arr[scan];
            }
        }
        while (write < arr.length) {
            arr[write++] = 0;
        }
    }
}
```

```text
Before: [500, 0, 300, 0, 0, 700, 100, 0]
After:  [500, 300, 700, 100, 0, 0, 0, 0]
```

### How does the two-pass write-and-fill approach work?

Pass 1: a write index starts at 0 and a scan index walks the entire array. Each time the scan finds a non-zero value, it copies it to the write position and advances the write pointer. After this pass, the first `write` positions hold all non-zero values in their original order.

Pass 2: fill every position from the write index to the end with zero.

```java
static void moveZerosTwoPass(int[] arr) {
    // Pass 1: compact non-zeros to the front
    int write = 0;
    for (int scan = 0; scan < arr.length; scan++) {
        if (arr[scan] != 0) {
            arr[write] = arr[scan];
            write++;
        }
    }
    // Pass 2: fill remaining with zeros
    for (int i = write; i < arr.length; i++) {
        arr[i] = 0;
    }
}
```

### How does the one-pass swap variant work?

Instead of a fill pass, swap each non-zero value with the value at the write position. This moves zeros rightward as you go. It does more writes than the fill version when zeros are frequent because of the swaps.

```java
static void moveZerosSwap(int[] arr) {
    int write = 0;
    for (int scan = 0; scan < arr.length; scan++) {
        if (arr[scan] != 0) {
            int temp = arr[write];
            arr[write] = arr[scan];
            arr[scan] = temp;
            write++;
        }
    }
}
```

```java
package com.bank.core;

import java.util.Arrays;

public class SwapVariantDemo {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        int[] a = {0, 0, 1, 0, 3, 12};
        System.out.println("Before: " + Arrays.toString(a));
        moveZerosSwap(a);
        System.out.println("After:  " + Arrays.toString(a));
    }
}
```

```text
Before: [0, 0, 1, 0, 3, 12]
After:  [1, 3, 12, 0, 0, 0]
```

### Why is stability important?

Stability means the non-zero values keep their original relative order. Both the write-and-fill and swap approaches preserve order because they process elements left to right and never reorder two non-zeros relative to each other. A two-ends approach that swaps from both ends would be faster at moving zeros but would scramble the non-zero order.

```java
// WRONG: two-ends swap breaks order
static void moveZerosWrong(int[] arr) {
    int left = 0, right = arr.length - 1;
    while (left < right) {
        if (arr[left] == 0 && arr[right] != 0) {
            int temp = arr[left];
            arr[left] = arr[right];
            arr[right] = temp;
        }
        if (arr[left] != 0) left++;
        if (arr[right] == 0) right--;
    }
}
// Input:  [500, 0, 300, 0, 0, 700, 100, 0]
// Output: [500, 100, 300, 700, 0, 0, 0, 0]  <-- 100 moved before 300!
```

### How does this work with BigDecimal for money?

For banking, amounts are `BigDecimal` and zero is checked with `signum()` rather than `==`. The same algorithm applies with the type change.

```java
package com.bank.core;

import java.math.BigDecimal;
import java.util.Arrays;

public class MoveZerosBigDecimal {

    static void moveZeros(BigDecimal[] arr) {
        int write = 0;
        for (int scan = 0; scan < arr.length; scan++) {
            if (arr[scan].signum() != 0) {
                arr[write++] = arr[scan];
            }
        }
        while (write < arr.length) {
            arr[write++] = BigDecimal.ZERO;
        }
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        BigDecimal[] amounts = {
            new BigDecimal("500.00"), BigDecimal.ZERO,
            new BigDecimal("300.00"), BigDecimal.ZERO,
            new BigDecimal("700.00")
        };
        System.out.println("Before: " + Arrays.toString(amounts));
        moveZeros(amounts);
        System.out.println("After:  " + Arrays.toString(amounts));
    }
}
```

```text
Before: [500.00, 0, 300.00, 0, 700.00]
After:  [500.00, 300.00, 700.00, 0, 0]
```

### How does this generalize to a stable partition?

The same pattern is a stable partition around any predicate, not just zero. Replace the non-zero test with any condition and you push all failing elements to the end while keeping the passing ones in order. This is a reusable building block.

```java
package com.bank.core;

import java.util.Arrays;

public class StablePartition {

    // Partition: move all negative values to the end, keep order
    static void partitionPositive(int[] arr) {
        int write = 0;
        for (int scan = 0; scan < arr.length; scan++) {
            if (arr[scan] >= 0) {
                arr[write++] = arr[scan];
            }
        }
        // In real use, store the negatives in a temp buffer
        // This simplified version just demonstrates the pattern
        while (write < arr.length) {
            arr[write++] = -1;  // placeholder
        }
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        int[] arr = {3, -1, 5, -2, 0, -4, 7};
        System.out.println("Before: " + Arrays.toString(arr));
        partitionPositive(arr);
        System.out.println("After:  " + Arrays.toString(arr));
    }
}
```

### What is the self-write optimization?

If the array is mostly non-zero, the write pointer and scan pointer move together, causing the algorithm to write a value back onto itself. Skipping the store when write equals scan avoids redundant writes and slightly reduces memory traffic on large arrays.

```java
static void moveZerosOptimized(int[] arr) {
    int write = 0;
    for (int scan = 0; scan < arr.length; scan++) {
        if (arr[scan] != 0) {
            if (write != scan) {
                arr[write] = arr[scan];
            }
            write++;
        }
    }
    while (write < arr.length) {
        arr[write++] = 0;
    }
}
```

### What are the edge cases?

Edge cases include an empty array, an array with no zeros, an array that is all zeros, and a single-element array. All are handled correctly by the write-and-fill approach without special checks.

```java
package com.bank.core;

import java.util.Arrays;

public class MoveZerosEdgeCases {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        int[] empty = {};
        int[] noZeros = {1, 2, 3};
        int[] allZeros = {0, 0, 0};
        int[] single = {0};

        moveZeros(empty);    System.out.println(Arrays.toString(empty));
        moveZeros(noZeros);  System.out.println(Arrays.toString(noZeros));
        moveZeros(allZeros); System.out.println(Arrays.toString(allZeros));
        moveZeros(single);   System.out.println(Arrays.toString(single));
    }

    static void moveZeros(int[] arr) {
        int w = 0;
        for (int s = 0; s < arr.length; s++) if (arr[s] != 0) arr[w++] = arr[s];
        while (w < arr.length) arr[w++] = 0;
    }
}
```

```text
[]
[1, 2, 3]
[0, 0, 0]
[0]
```

### What should a senior engineer know?

- **Write count comparison.** The two-pass version writes each non-zero once and each trailing zero once. The swap version writes each swap pair, which is more writes when zeros are frequent but fewer when zeros are rare.
- **Predicate generalization.** This is the foundation of stable partition algorithms used in sorting and data pipelines.
- **In-place constraint.** Allocating a second array and filtering is trivially correct but disqualifying for the interview, since the whole point is to demonstrate in-place modification.
- **Memory traffic.** On very large arrays in batch processing, the self-write optimization measurably reduces cache-line flushes.
